#=====================================================================
# test/diag/tdgctl.ref
#
# Reference-value data for PROGRAM TDGCTL (test/diag/tdgctl.f).
#
# Routine under test : modern/diag/diagctl.f
# Reference source   : bin/nastrn.f (GETENV idiom, LOUT=3);
#                      bin/nastran  (csh env-var toggle convention)
#
# DELIVERABLE -- used in Solaris; not executed by Blitzy.
# This file is reference DATA only.  It is NOT a *.f source and is NOT
# matched by the test/run_units.csh "*.f" build glob, so it is never
# compiled or linked.  It is read/inspected by the unit driver and the
# test harness in the developer's Solaris environment (csh + Sun f77).
#
# Expected verdict   : PASS: TDGCTL
#=====================================================================
#
# PURPOSE
#   TDGCTL verifies TOGGLE WIRING and the DEFAULT-SILENT (APR.95)
#   behavior of modern/diag/diagctl.f.  DIAGCTL reads the NASTRAN_*
#   environment toggles via GETENV and computes the diagnostics-enable
#   state IDIAG once (returned as the output argument of
#   SUBROUTINE DIAGCTL (IDIAG)).  The central safety / rollback
#   guarantee is: with NO toggle set, IDIAG = 0, every guarded modern/
#   routine short-circuits, and the run is bit-identical to the
#   unmodified APR.95 solver.  This reference encodes the toggle set,
#   the env -> IDIAG state mapping, and the expected driver verdict.
#
# CONFIGURATION TOGGLES (read by DIAGCTL via GETENV; default = unset)
#   The exact subset that drives IDIAG is DEFINED BY
#   modern/diag/diagctl.f; this list is aligned with that source.
#   DIAGCTL contributes one bit to IDIAG for each toggle that is set
#   to a non-blank value (the bin/nastrn.f GETENV idiom: an unset
#   variable comes back blank, so blank/unset => disabled):
#
#     NASTRAN_LEGACY_INIT        bit value 1
#     NASTRAN_INIT_VALIDATE      bit value 2
#     NASTRAN_DISPATCH_VALIDATE  bit value 4
#     NASTRAN_DISPATCH_LOG       bit value 8
#
#   IDIAG = sum of the bit values of the toggles that are set.
#
# EXPECTED STATE MAPPING (env -> IDIAG -> behavior)
#   all toggles unset          => IDIAG = 0  => SILENT / APR.95
#                                 default (zero diagnostic records on
#                                 logical unit 3 / LOUT).
#   any relevant NASTRAN_* set => IDIAG non-zero => ENABLED (the
#                                 guard clause passes; DIAGLOG emits
#                                 to logical unit 3).
#
#   Representative combinations (for human review):
#     (none set)                    => IDIAG = 0   (silent)
#     NASTRAN_LEGACY_INIT set       => IDIAG = 1   (enabled)
#     NASTRAN_INIT_VALIDATE set     => IDIAG = 2   (enabled)
#     NASTRAN_DISPATCH_VALIDATE set => IDIAG = 4   (enabled)
#     NASTRAN_DISPATCH_LOG set      => IDIAG = 8   (enabled)
#     all four toggles set          => IDIAG = 15  (enabled)
#
# RATIONALE (evidence from the reference sources)
#   * bin/nastrn.f establishes the NASTRAN configuration idiom:
#     environment variables read at startup via GETENV into a
#     CHARACTER*80 receiver (VALUE=' ' then CALL GETENV(name,VALUE));
#     an unset variable returns all blanks.  The modern NASTRAN_*
#     toggles follow this same idiom (non-blank => set/enabled).
#   * bin/nastrn.f makes logical unit 3 the NASTRAN log: after the
#     bootstrap CALL DBMINT (L44) it sets LOUT = 3 (L45) -- the only
#     sink a modern diagnostic write may reach.
#   * bin/nastran (csh wrapper) passes env vars inline before
#     nastrn.exe; that is where operators add the NASTRAN_* toggles to
#     enable diagnostics.  Rollback to APR.95 is therefore a pure
#     configuration change (unset the toggle) with no recompilation.
#
# BINDING EXPECTATION
#   In a clean environment (the runner sets only LOGNM, no NASTRAN_*),
#   after CALL DIAGCTL(IDIAG) the state is DISABLED: IDIAG = 0.  The
#   driver confirms default-silence behaviorally -- a subsequent
#   DIAGLOG call writes ZERO records to logical unit 3 because the
#   guard clause IF (IDIAG .EQ. 0) RETURN short-circuits before any
#   I/O -- and then emits the verdict PASS: TDGCTL to logical unit 3.
#
# CONSISTENCY
#   DEFAULT_IDIAG below MUST match the assertion hardcoded in
#   test/diag/tdgctl.f (clean env => IDIAG = 0 / silent) and the
#   env -> IDIAG mapping in modern/diag/diagctl.f.  ALL_SET_IDIAG and
#   TOGGLE_COUNT reflect the four-toggle bitmask above.  If the driver
#   author and the modern/diag/diagctl.f author finalize a different
#   toggle subset, update both the driver and this reference to agree.
#
#---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-readable key/value lines (whitespace separated, one per
# line).  These are the values the driver and harness parse.
#---------------------------------------------------------------------
DEFAULT_IDIAG 0
TOGGLE_COUNT 4
ALL_SET_IDIAG 15
EXPECT_VERDICT PASS: TDGCTL
